# Orrery — Showcase

The finished game ships a showcase: the store-page presentation a player reads
before deciding to play. It is a `showcase/` directory at the root of this
repository, committed with the rest of the project, holding a description of
the game and a short carousel of captured media. This file defines its
contents.

## Layout

Every file of the showcase sits directly in `showcase/`, with no
subdirectories:

- `showcase/showcase.md`, the description.
- `showcase/showcase.toml`, the carousel.
- The media files the carousel and the description name.

## The description

`showcase/showcase.md` is Markdown written for a player: what Orrery is, how a
challenge is taken from an empty field to a machine that delivers its
constellations, and what Campaign and Extras each offer. Three to five short
paragraphs, between 200 and 500 words in all. It is strictly about playing the
game; implementation, architecture, and tooling stay out of it. It may embed
images that sit beside it, referenced by bare relative path (for example
`![The field](field.png)`); every image it embeds is a `.png` in `showcase/`.

## The carousel

`showcase/showcase.toml` lists the media in the order they are presented, one
`[[media]]` table per entry:

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
```toml
[[media]]
file = "building-a-machine.webm"
name = "A machine built, run, and delivering"

[[media]]
file = "field.png"
name = "Arms carrying motes mid-run"
```
{{else}}
```toml
[[media]]
file = "building-a-machine.json.gz"
name = "A machine built, run, and delivering"

[[media]]
file = "field.png"
name = "Arms carrying motes mid-run"
```
{{/if}}

- `file` names a file directly in `showcase/`, with no path separators. Every
  file the carousel names exists there, and each stays under 25 MiB.
- `name` is a caption of a few words, under sixty characters.
- The carousel holds three to five entries: one moving entry first, then two
  to four stills.

## What the media shows

The leading entry is a single continuous stretch of live play, fifteen
seconds or longer, of one challenge worked in the editor and then run: parts
taken from the tray onto the field, at least one tape written in the tape
panel, the machine started from the run controls, arms turning and carrying
motes across the field, and at least one constellation delivered at a set. It
ends on the completed run or the solved panel rather than mid-swing.

The stills are frames of that same game running, such as the field mid-run
with the tape panel beside it, the solved panel with its three metrics, or
the title screen. Each still holds the whole stage, at `1280 x 720` pixels or
larger.

The machine on screen is built and run through the game's own editor and run
controls, and everything it does follows from the simulation of
`specs/simulation.md` under that input. What a player sees is the built game
drawing the sprites, sheets, and effects this build produced, so the showcase
presents the finished game as it plays.

## Media

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The file extension states what an entry is.

| Extension | Media |
| --- | --- |
| `.png` | A screenshot of the running game. |
| `.webm` | A video clip of the running game. |

The leading entry is a `.webm` clip recorded at 30 frames a second or better,
and the stills are `.png`. How screenshots and clips are captured is the
build's own business.
{{else}}
The file extension states what an entry is.

| Extension | Media |
| --- | --- |
| `.png` | A screenshot of the running game. |
| `.json.gz` | A replay: a gzipped recording of the game's own drawing, made with the engine's recorder. |
| `.webm` | A video clip. |

Anything in motion is captured as a replay rather than a video.

### Capturing a replay

A replay is a gzipped JSON recording made with the engine's recorder, which
the engine documents in `engine/recording.md` at the root of this repository.
The leading entry is one such recording, covering the stretch of play above in
a single unbroken session at the game's own frame rate. How screenshots and
replays are captured is the build's own business.
{{/if}}
